/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin

# Why these three headers exist
#
# Together they put the page in a cross-origin isolated context, which is the
# precondition for SharedArrayBuffer and therefore for multi-threaded WASM.
# Without them onnxruntime-web runs single-threaded and speech recognition is
# several times slower — the difference between usable and unusable on iOS,
# where WebGPU is still behind a Safari feature flag and WASM is all we have.
#
# This file is read by Cloudflare Pages and Netlify. GitHub Pages ignores it
# and offers no way to set headers at all, which is the reason this project
# does not deploy there.
#
# Consequence to be aware of: with COEP require-corp, every cross-origin
# subresource must opt in via CORP/CORS. That is fine here because after setup
# the app loads nothing cross-origin at all.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three files that decide whether an update is ever seen.
#
# An installed PWA serves its shell from the service worker's precache. The
# browser only notices a new build when it re-fetches sw.js and finds it
# changed — and if sw.js itself is served from the HTTP cache, that never
# happens. The app then stays frozen on an old build no matter how many times
# it is force-closed and reopened, which is exactly what went wrong here.
#
# These rules keep the entry points uncacheable. The hashed assets they point
# at stay cacheable, so this costs almost nothing.

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

/registerSW.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache
